rrent-text-info)}\\n        }\\n    \",ticketButtonText:\"Get tickets\"},settings:{afterCheckoutRedirect:void 0,allowTicketAndMerchSwaps:false,allowUserEdit:true,codesViaLinkOnly:false,disableHumanitixGTM:false,hideAddToCalendar:false,hideArtistPreviews:false,removeImpactCommunication:false,requireEmailConfirmation:false,sendTickets:true,noTicketsMessagingOption:\"none\",showTicketsLeft:false,minimumTicketsPerOrder:void 0,maximumTicketsPerOrder:void 0,seatingMapEditable:false,enableV3SeatMaps:false,waitlistEnabled:false,salesStopped:false,hideConfetti:false,mobileRequired:true},eventModules:[{component:\"richtext\",props:{title:\"Description\",content:\"\\u003Cp>In the bloodiest instalment of his hit Terror Talks series, dark historian Andrew Johnston (Alternative Ulstours) takes a startling new look at history’s most elusive serial killer – Jack the Ripper.\\u003C/p>\\u003Cp>Belfast played a surprisingly intense role during 1888’s ‘Autumn of Terror’. Hysterical press coverage of the slayings in London led to horrific copycat attacks in Ulster, with vengeful Irish mobs targeting alleged ‘Rippers’.\\u003C/p>\\u003Cp>Amidst this, a fugitive Englishman was arrested, charged and imprisoned in Belfast on suspicion of being ‘Jack’. The compelling case of William John Foster – dubbed Belfast’s ‘man with the knives’ – unfolded between the fourth and fifth canonical Ripper murders.\\u003C/p>\\u003Cp>Did Belfast have the Whitechapel fiend in custody, and did local authorities let him slip through their fingers?\\u003C/p>\\u003Cp>Andrew’s entertaining lecture will also shine a flashlight on a suspect with family ties to a venerated unionist politician, as well as exploring Belfast’s links to a potential Royal ‘Ripper’.\\u003C/p>\"}}],artists:[],seo:{jsonSchema:[{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"Event\",name:\"Jack the Ripper: From Hell to Belfast\",url:\"https://events.humanitix.com/jack-the-ripper-from-hell-to-belfast\",startDate:\"2026-10-11T15:00:00+0100\",en